WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:10.794
Hi! Why don't fridge magnets fall off? Because they contain magnets. Today let's review magnets from Primary 3.

2
00:00:10.944 --> 00:00:18.786
Every magnet has two poles: a north pole and a south pole. Its magnetism is strongest at the poles.

3
00:00:18.936 --> 00:00:24.162
When two magnets come close, a north pole and a south pole attract each other.

4
00:00:24.312 --> 00:00:29.394
Two north poles, or two south poles, repel each other and push apart.

5
00:00:29.544 --> 00:00:39.402
Hang a magnet from a thread so it can turn freely. When it stops, it always points north and south. This is how a compass works.

6
00:00:39.552 --> 00:00:47.106
Magnets attract things made of iron, steel, nickel and cobalt, like an iron nail or a steel paper clip.

7
00:00:47.256 --> 00:00:57.018
An aluminium can, copper wire, plastic, wood, a rubber band and a paper cup are not attracted, because they are not magnetic materials.

8
00:00:57.168 --> 00:01:09.090
Magnets are useful. They keep fridge doors shut, a compass helps us find directions, and electromagnet cranes in scrapyards lift heavy pieces of iron.

9
00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:19.050
Stroke a steel rod many times with one pole of a magnet, always in the same direction. The rod becomes a magnet, and more strokes make it stronger.

10
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:29.178
Coil a wire round an iron nail and connect it to batteries. The nail becomes an electromagnet. More coils or more batteries make it stronger.

11
00:01:29.328 --> 00:01:37.362
If you use a copper rod instead, it does not become a magnet, because copper is not a magnetic material.

12
00:01:37.512 --> 00:01:41.778
Watch out! Magnets do not attract all metals!

13
00:01:41.928 --> 00:01:47.322
An aluminium can and copper wire are metals, but a magnet won't pick them up.

14
00:01:47.472 --> 00:01:53.778
Only things made of iron, steel, nickel or cobalt are attracted.

15
00:01:53.928 --> 00:02:06.522
Your turn! Rod X is brought near a magnet. One end attracts it and the other end repels it. What is X? Pause and think.

16
00:02:06.672 --> 00:02:16.866
X is a magnet. Only a magnet can repel another magnet. An iron rod would only be attracted. Did you get it right?

17
00:02:17.016 --> 00:02:27.282
Another one! Give two ways to make an electromagnet pick up more paper clips. Pause and think.

18
00:02:27.432 --> 00:02:34.506
Wind more coils of wire round the nail, or use more batteries. Did you get it right?

19
00:02:34.656 --> 00:02:45.306
Last one! Will a piece of aluminium foil be attracted by a magnet? Why? Pause and think.

20
00:02:45.456 --> 00:02:53.442
No. It is made of aluminium, which is not a magnetic material. Did you get it right?

21
00:02:53.592 --> 00:03:04.938
Remember: a magnet has a north and a south pole. Like poles repel and unlike poles attract. Magnets only attract iron, steel, nickel and cobalt.

22
00:03:05.088 --> 00:03:10.458
We can make a magnet by stroking in one direction, or by using electricity.

23
00:03:10.608 --> 00:03:16.098
At home, try a fridge magnet and see what it sticks to!

